1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,562
Day 57: QueryLM, a 360 million parameter model pretrained from

2
00:00:05,562 --> 00:00:10,495
scratch to write search queries, tested on a local CPU.

3
00:00:10,945 --> 00:00:14,653
QueryLM was pretrained on Wikipedia text where some positions

4
00:00:14,653 --> 00:00:18,118
were replaced with a search query followed by a retrieved

5
00:00:18,118 --> 00:00:18,604
passage.

6
00:00:18,604 --> 00:00:21,157
The model only learned to write the query,

7
00:00:21,157 --> 00:00:23,345
never to predict the passage itself.

8
00:00:23,795 --> 00:00:27,878
Given a question, the model can open a search block and

9
00:00:27,878 --> 00:00:30,772
write its own query, at most 24 tokens.

10
00:00:31,222 --> 00:00:33,144
The caller runs any retriever and

11
00:00:33,144 --> 00:00:35,415
inserts the passage back into the text.

12
00:00:35,415 --> 00:00:38,908
The model was never trained to generate that passage itself.

13
00:00:39,358 --> 00:00:42,946
With the passage in context, the model keeps generating and

14
00:00:42,946 --> 00:00:44,223
answers the question.

15
00:00:44,673 --> 00:00:46,425
On 8 knowledge questions,

16
00:00:46,425 --> 00:00:50,210
QueryLM beat the standard baseline 3 to 0 even without

17
00:00:50,210 --> 00:00:50,911
searching.

18
00:00:50,911 --> 00:00:54,486
But letting it actually search sometimes backfired:

19
00:00:54,486 --> 00:00:58,552
two answers it had right turned into an empty string or an

20
00:00:58,552 --> 00:00:59,603
unrelated word.

21
00:01:00,053 --> 00:01:02,553
Chat fine-tuning fixed this instability.

22
00:01:02,553 --> 00:01:05,615
Full results and sources are in the Zenn article,

23
00:01:05,615 --> 00:01:06,427
linked below.
